    {
      "type": "base_rate",
      "text": "Outside view: official echoed comparable first-print-like line-item sample is 2022 = 17.219508 million and 2023 = 17.401584 million Schedule 8812 ACTC line 27 returns, a +0.182076 million one-year change. Last-print persistence prior is 17.4 million; sparse two-point drift to 2027 gives 17.401584 + 4 * 0.182076 = 18.129888 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "model_prior",
      "text": "Model candidates: persistence is the strongest auditable benchmark because only two fully echoed comparable official values were available; a two-point drift model is admissible only as a weak directional candidate. I use a 75% persistence and 25% drift blend: 0.75 * 17.401584 + 0.25 * 18.129888 = 17.583660 million before current-law adjustment."
    },
    {
      "type": "math",
      "text": "Point math: benchmark blend 17.583660 million. Current-law mechanics add a modest +0.2 million because IRS now describes the CTC as up to $2,200 and ACTC up to $1,700 with the same $2,500 earnings threshold, which should weakly expand positive ACTC claims relative to the 2023 Schedule 8812 regime but is already limited by child counts, filing behavior, and tax liability. Rounded point = 17.8 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "math",
      "text": "Interval math: observed 2022-2023 movement is 0.182076 million, but the calibration sample is only two tax years and the target is four years ahead under a post-2025 law regime. I use an explicit sparse-history fallback: base four-year drift uncertainty 0.182076 * sqrt(4) = 0.364152 million, plus structural/policy implementation uncertainty 0.65 million; combined sigma = sqrt(0.364152^2 + 0.65^2) = 0.745 million. 80% normal half-width = 1.28 * 0.745 = 0.954 million, widened 1.35x for sparse history and IRS sampling/release uncertainty = 1.29 million. Around 17.8, rounded 80% interval = 16.6 to 19.2 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "prior_update_interval",
      "text": "Prior/update/interval: prior is last-print persistence at 17.4 million from 2023 IRS Schedule 8812 ACTC line 27, with a weak two-point drift candidate from the 2022-2023 official sample; update components are +0.18 million trend pull and +0.2 million current-law refundable-credit mechanics; interval method is sparse-history four-year uncertainty widened for structural and sampling risk, implying 16.6 to 19.2 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "counter_consideration",
      "text": "Downside tail outside 16.6 million: weaker low-income employment among families with qualifying children, fewer eligible children with required SSNs, or IRS filing/compliance changes reduce positive ACTC line 27 returns despite the threshold staying at $2,500. Upside tail outside 19.2 million: stronger employment and filing participation among lower-income families, more returns limited by tax liability after the larger CTC, or outreach/compliance changes produce broader ACTC claiming without a statutory threshold change."
